<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johann Gottfried Herder</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1744">1744</a>–<a href="/years/1803">1803</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Herder</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johann Gottfried Herder was a German philosopher, theologian and writer, born in Mohrungen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a>. A leading figure of the Sturm und Drang and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> Classicism, he argued that each people has its own language, culture and character, and he collected folk songs from many nations. From 1776 he was the chief Lutheran clergyman of Saxe-Weimar, where he worked alongside Goethe, Wieland and Schiller.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johann-gottfried-herder.jpg" alt="Johann Gottfried Herder" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anton Graff · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johann_Gottfried_Herder_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Johann Gottfried Herder was born on 25 August 1744 in Mohrungen, now Morąg in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a>, the son of a teacher. He grew up in a poor household and educated himself from his father's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> and songbook. In 1762, at seventeen, he enrolled at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-konigsberg">University of Königsberg</a>, where he studied under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/immanuel-kant">Immanuel Kant</a> and became a protégé of the philosopher Johann Georg Hamann, who disputed the claims of pure secular reason.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1764 Herder went to Riga as a Lutheran pastor and teacher, and there he wrote his first major works, which were literary criticism. In September 1770 he met the young Goethe at Strasbourg, whom his criticism inspired to develop his own style, which can be seen as the beginning of the Sturm und Drang movement. In 1771 he became head pastor and court preacher at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buckeburg">Bückeburg</a>. By the mid-1770s Goethe, by then a well-known author, used his influence at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> to secure Herder the post of General Superintendent, and Herder moved there in 1776, where his outlook shifted towards classicism.</p><p>Late in his career he endorsed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, which earned him the enmity of many colleagues, and he and Goethe experienced a personal split. He was ennobled in 1802, and he died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> in 1803, aged 59.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Herder was one of the first to argue that language shapes the patterns by which each linguistic community thinks and feels. In his Treatise on the Origin of Language of 1772 he defined language as the natural organ of the understanding. His most ambitious work, the four-volume Ideas for a Philosophy of the History of Mankind, was published between 1784 and 1791 and is regarded as an origin of the modern school of historical thought.</p><p>Germans had no nation-state until the nineteenth century, and Herder was among the first German intellectuals to base a national consciousness mainly on language and literature. He taught that every nation carries its own standard of perfection, and that the true spirit of a people lies in its folk songs, poetry and dances. He collected folk songs, published in 1773 as Voices of the Peoples in Their Songs, which Achim von Arnim and Clemens von Brentano later drew on for The Boy's Magic Horn. He refused a rigid racial theory, writing that there is only one species of man throughout the whole earth.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Johann Gottfried Herder” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Herders</b> took livestock to the uplands each season.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vinca-culture">Vinča culture · Society</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Johann Gottfried Herder</b> taught that every people has its own spirit, expressed in its language and folk songs.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Johann Gottfried Herder</b> applied the German Stadtstaat to the polis in 1765, and the English historian W. Warde Fowler introduced “city-state” in 1893 for “a state in which the whole life and energy of the people, political, intellectual, religious, is focused at one point, and that point a…</blockquote><a href="/government/city-state">City-state · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Prussian scholar <b>Johann Gottfried Herder</b> originated the term in 1772 in his Treatise on the Origin of Language, stressing a common language; Erica Benner calls him the first philosopher to suggest that identities based on language should be the primary source of legitimate political authority.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/nationalism">Nationalism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>He held a Romantic idea of Irish identity, shaped by Johann Gottfried von <b>Herder</b>: Ireland was a spiritual reality grounded in historic culture, and anyone who took it as their homeland, whatever their religion, was Irish.</blockquote><a href="/songs/a-nation-once-again">A Nation Once Again</a></li><li><blockquote>The theologian Jaime Pérez de Valencia (1408–1490) read its ninth verse as confirming Ptolemy's view that the oceans were surrounded by land, and <b>Johann Gottfried Herder</b> remarked that it was worth studying Hebrew for ten years to read it in the original.</blockquote><a href="/songs/psalm-104">Psalm 104</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Morąg, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.92,19.93&amp;year=1782" aria-label="Morąg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4992 313 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Johann Gottfried Herder"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5442 583h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5442 583h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.92,19.93&amp;year=1782">What was here in 1782? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
